response.output:
In the Akka SDK you can't wire agent A to call agent B as a tool. Chaining agents as tools is an anti-pattern there. Instead, have a Workflow call both agents in separate steps, with the same session id so they share context.

```java
@Component(id = "a-then-b")
public class AThenBWorkflow extends Workflow<State> {
  public Effect<Done> start(String msg) {
    return effects().updateState(State.of(msg)).transitionTo("askA").thenReply(Done.getInstance());
  }

  @Override
  public WorkflowDef<State> definition() {
    var askA = step("askA")
      .call(() -> componentClient.forAgent().inSession(sessionId())
          .method(AgentA::query).invoke(currentState().msg()))
      .andThen(String.class, a -> effects().updateState(currentState().withA(a)).transitionTo("askB"));

    var askB = step("askB")
      .call(() -> componentClient.forAgent().inSession(sessionId())
          .method(AgentB::query).invoke(currentState().a()))
      .andThen(String.class, b -> effects().updateState(currentState().withB(b)).end());

    return workflow().addStep(askA).addStep(askB)
      .defaultStepTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(60));   // AI calls are slow
  }
}
```

- **Session id:** use the workflow id as the session id, so A and B share memory.
- **Timeouts and recovery:** set a step timeout and a bounded recovery strategy so a slow model call can't retry forever.
- **Runtime choice of B:** if A has to decide which agent runs, use `AgentRegistry` with `dynamicCall` from the workflow. A can return a structured response (`responseConformsTo`) that names the next agent.
- **Exposing B's logic as a tool:** if you really want a tool that A calls, put the logic in a function tool or an MCP tool, not in another agent.

I'm assuming you mean the Akka SDK, since this directory is `akka-sdk-agents`. If you meant a different framework, such as Claude Agent SDK subagents, tell me and I'll answer for that instead.

